$DIR/main.p:55:2: cannot pass &wg to sync.(*WaitGroup).Task, which starts a task with it: the new task may outlive wg
    &wg is a pointer to wg. wg is a local variable of waitGroup (declared at
    main.p:54). The task started here runs on its own, and may still be running
    after waitGroup returns and wg's memory is reclaimed.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Declare wg at package level, so that it outlives every task:
        var wg sync.WaitGroup // at package level
    Or start the tasks with a sync.Scope, which waits for them before its block
    ends, so that they may use the function's variables:
        var s sync.Scope
        s.Task(func() { ... })
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3004" for a longer explanation)

